Cursor is the implementation agent for this repo. Claude works the same local
working copy as technical lead: it plans, writes tasks, and reviews what you
built. This file covers only the handoff between them.

## Handoff

`.ai/current-task.md` is the mailbox. "check your mail", "check the mailbox",
and "what's in the mailbox?" all mean this file — read it and act on its
`Owner` and `Status`. When its Owner is `cursor` and Status is
`ready-for-cursor`:

1. Read the task. If it is ambiguous, contradicts `AGENTS.md`, or needs a
   decision rather than an implementation, set Status `ready-for-review` with
   the question and stop — do not guess.
2. **Verify the Premises block before writing anything.** Run every command it
   lists. Claude is required to have run them already, so this is confirmation,
   not discovery — and it is cheap.
   - A premise that turns out **false** → set Status `ready-for-review` naming
     which one and what you actually observed, and **stop**. Do not adapt the
     task around it. A false premise means the task is wrong, not that the
     implementation needs to be creative.
   - A **missing or empty** Premises block → report it as a defect and stop.
     It means the task was written without verifying its own claims. This is
     not a formality: it is the check that catches Claude cutting corners, and
     Claude cannot be the one to enforce it.
3. Set Status `in-progress`.
4. Implement exactly that scope. No unrelated refactors, no reformatting
   untouched files (`AGENTS.md` → Task loop).
5. Run the checks the task names, plus `npm run check`.
6. Record it, per the task's Mode — the task file is not a substitute for
   either record:
   - **product** — update `docs/build-status.md`.
   - **template** — leave `docs/build-status.md` and every
     `<!-- TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER -->` doc untouched unless the task explicitly
     changes the template's documentation structure. Update
     `REPO-EVALUATION.md` instead, moving the item from §2/§3 to §1 only once
     the proof that file requires has actually been established.
7. Fill the Implementation report, set Owner `claude`, Status
   `ready-for-review`. Report deviations and blockers honestly; Claude reads
   the diff, so an omission surfaces as a discrepancy.
8. **Last, after the mailbox is completely written — never before, never in the
   same breath —** rewrite `.ai/mailbox-state.json` with `seq` incremented by
   one, `owner` `claude`, `status` `ready-for-review`, and `mode` unchanged.
   That bump is how Claude learns the work is ready. Bumping it early, or
   instead of finishing the report, hands Claude a half-written report to
   review.

## Proposing a better approach

Claude writes the task, but Claude is not always right — a task has reached you
inverted, aimed at the wrong file, and carrying an instruction that deleted the
very log its own error message told the user to read. You are the last check
before that becomes committed code. Say something.

**Propose; do not substitute.** Never silently implement a different design
than the one the task specifies, however sure you are. Claude owns architecture,
purchase and entitlement logic, and design trade-offs (`AGENTS.md` → *Delegate
to a cheaper model*). Your alternative is a recommendation until it is reissued
as a task.

Timing:

- **The plan is wrong, or your alternative is materially better** — set Status
  `ready-for-review` **before building**, describe both, and stop. Wasted
  round-trips are cheap; wrong code in a product clone is not.
- **Minor preference, task is sound** — build it as specified, then note the
  alternative under Deviations. Do not stall a correct task over taste.

Make the case checkable, not aesthetic. "Fewer moving parts", "removes a
failure mode", "drops a dependency", "the existing helper already does this"
are arguments. "Cleaner" and "more idiomatic" are not. Name the concrete
failure the current plan allows, or the concrete thing yours removes.

"Better" means a better route to the **same** goal. It is not a licence to widen
scope, refactor neighbouring code, or solve a problem the task did not ask
about — `AGENTS.md` → *Task loop* still governs.

If you only realise after building, say so in the report: what you built, what
you would have done, and why. That is still useful; it is not a failure.

If the user explicitly asks for a specific small change, do it and run
`npm run check`. If you are not sure it qualifies as small, ask first.

`.ai/mailbox-state.json` is the delivery signal both ways. Never read it as task
content. Write it in exactly one situation: step 7 above, on completion. Both
agents follow the same discipline — write the mailbox first, bump the signal
last.

`.ai/.watching` and `.ai/.review-seen` belong to the watchers. Never read or
write them; editing or deleting one mid-flight breaks the handoff. `/watch` and
`/stop` own `.watching`, the hooks own the rest.

When Owner is `claude`, do not write source files.
